DATA_MODE



char DATA_MODE(N_ CYCLE);


DATA_MODE:long_name = “Delayed mode or real time data”;


DATA_MODE:conventions = “R : real time; D : delayed mode; A : real time with adjustment”;


DATA_MODE:_FillValue = ” “;




Indicates if the trajectory cycle contains real time, adjusted or delayed mode data. A delayed mode cycle means the positions, times, cycle number, pressure, temperature, and salinity (if measured) have been quality controlled. Additional parameters like oxygen may not be quality controlled. Floats often have delayed mode data only after they die, but can have both delayed mode and real time data for different parameters while alive. When this occurs, two trajectory files exist - a real time file (“R”) with only real time data for all the cycles in the float record and a delayed mode file (“D”) with both real time and delayed mode data for all the cycles that have been delayed mode quality controlled. Floats can be adjusted in real time with adjusted time values only in the JULD_ADJUSTED variable and its associated _STATUS and _QC variables. This occurs when floats are corrected in real time for clock drift.


Examples :


‘R’ : real time data


‘D’ : delayed mode data


‘A’ : real time data with JULD_ADJUSTED values





ICE_ALGORITHM_STATUS


integer ICE_ALGORITHM_STATUS(N_CYCLE); ICE_ALGORITHM_STATUS:long_name=“Status of the results of the on-board ice avoidance algorithm mapped to standardised categories”; ICE_ALGORITHM_STATUS:conventions=“Argo reference table 42” ICE_ALGORITHM_STATUS:_FillValue= 99999;



This is an optional variable.


The ICE_ALGORITHM_STATUS variable does not directly report the value transmitted by the float as these values vary by float model. These float-specific values can be found in the tech-netCDF.


Instead, the ICE_ALGORITHM_STATUS is applicable to all float types, matching what is transmitted to a list of common situations.


There are 2 options available to DACs on how to fill this variable, BASIC and EXTENDED.


Argo reference table R42 (soon on the NVS, see §3.35 in the meanwhile) references the general meanings of the basic and extended values, whatever the float type.


Specific and detailed lookup tables by float type are provided in the trajectory cookbook, which also specifies how to fill this variable from float-specific technical data.


The trajectory cookbook (https://dx.doi.org/10.13155/29824) must be followed to fill this variable

